<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nabu-apla-iddina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 888–855 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nabu-apla-iddina was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> from about 888 to 855 BCE, one of the longest reigns of the period. He restored temples damaged in earlier Aramean and Sutean raids, notably renewing the cult image of the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, an event recorded on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tablet-of-shamash">Sun-god Tablet</a>. He kept peaceful relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> through a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabu-apla-iddina.jpg" alt="Nabu-apla-iddina" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Geni Author of the tablet unknown · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nabu-apla-iddina_confirming_a_grant_of_land.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nabû-apla-iddina was the sixth king of the dynasty of E and the second of four generations of one family to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>: his father Nabû-šuma-ukin I preceded him and his son Marduk-zakir-šumi I followed. His reign came after a hundred and fifty years of instability in which Aramaean tribes had raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. The Suteans had sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> in the 11th century and so destroyed the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> that the cult image of Šamaš was lost, and a votive disc had served in its place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> was a powerful rival, but he avoided open war and any great loss of territory. In 878 BCE he sent troops to Suḫu on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> when its governor Kudurru refused tribute to Assyria; the army of 3,000, led by his brother Zabdanu and the diviner Bel-apli-iddina, was defeated and its leaders taken prisoner. Late in his reign the treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> helped to secure his son's rule against the revolt of Marduk-bēl-ušati. Kassites held high positions at his court, the province of Chaldea is first mentioned in his time, and the king's feathered crown gave way to a peaked dome.</p><p>The Sun God Tablet shows him led by the priest Nabû-nadin-shum and the goddess Aa before the seated Šamaš, and celebrates his victory over the Sutû. He was the first Babylonian king in over two centuries, since Nabû-kudurrī-uṣur I, to claim a military title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His reign saw something of a literary revival: new editions of the Utukkū Lemnūtu series and the Sakikkū texts were prepared, and Kabti-ilani-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a>'s Epic of Erra is sometimes dated to it. About 250 years later Nabû-apal-usur found the Sun God Tablet already broken and placed it in a clay box with his own inscription, where it was discovered in the 19th century. Inscriptions of his time survive on perhaps five kudurrus, and Assyrian king lists and chronicles name him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nabu-apla-iddina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nabu-apla-iddina</b> restored the cult of Shamash at Sippar, commemorated on the Sun-god Tablet.</blockquote><a href="/state/dynasty-of-e">Dynasty of E</a></li></ul></section>
